package com.shahaabapps.core.content

/**
 * The German A1 episodes, one for each topic. Maya explains in plain English
 * and Leo asks what a beginner would ask, as in the AZ-204 episodes; Jonas,
 * a native speaker, says every German word and sentence, so nothing a
 * learner copies is said with an English accent. A line of his that ends in
 * [repeat] is a drill: he says it slowly, leaves time to say it out loud,
 * then says it at normal speed. The transcript shows his German with the
 * English under it.
 *
 * Each episode teaches what its topic teaches and nothing else, ends with
 * what it means for the Goethe-Zertifikat A1 and the topic's own thing to try
 * today. GermanPodcastTest holds the shape; GermanGrammarTest reads Jonas's
 * German with everything else the course prints as correct.
 */
internal object GermanPodcasts {
    private val de1Words = Podcast(
        "de1-words",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome to the first German episode. Today is greetings: hello, goodbye, please and thank you. And we have a new voice. Jonas grew up speaking German, so every German word you hear comes from him. When he leaves a gap, say it out loud with him."),
            jonas("Hallo! Ich bin Jonas. Schön, dich kennenzulernen.", "Hello! I'm Jonas. Nice to meet you."),
            leo("So when Jonas leaves a gap, I really say it, out loud? Even on the bus?"),
            maya("[chuckle] Quietly on the bus is fine. Saying it is how it sticks. Let's start with the polite hello, the one for people you don't know."),
            jonas("Guten Tag. [repeat]", "Good day: the polite hello."),
            leo("When do I use «Guten Tag» instead of just «Hallo»?"),
            maya("German keeps polite and friendly further apart than English does. The polite way is called «formell», formal, and it goes with «Sie», the polite word for you. For example, you walk into a bank in Berlin, a shop, the immigration office, or you meet a new colleague. «Guten Tag», and «Sie»."),
            jonas("Hallo! [repeat]", "Hello."),
            leo("And «Hallo» is for friends?"),
            maya("Yes. The friendly way is «informell», informal, and it goes with «du», the friendly word for you. Friends, family, children, your partner. Say you're greeting Sanya at home. That's «Hallo», and a friendly how are you."),
            jonas("Hallo Sanya, wie geht's? [repeat]", "Hi Sanya, how are you?"),
            leo("And what does Sanya say back?"),
            maya("The easy answer is, I'm well, thank you. Literally, it goes well for me."),
            jonas("Mir geht es gut, danke. [repeat]", "I'm well, thank you."),
            leo("What about the time of day? English has good morning and good evening."),
            maya("So does German, and they line up nicely. Good morning, good evening, good night. Listen for the small change in the last one."),
            jonas("Guten Morgen. Guten Abend. Gute Nacht. [repeat]", "Good morning. Good evening. Good night."),
            leo("[laugh] So it's «Guten Abend» when I arrive for dinner, and «Gute Nacht» when I leave?"),
            maya("Exactly, and that's a mistake people make. «Guten Abend» greets someone. «Gute Nacht» is what you say on the way out at night. Now goodbyes. They follow the same split as hello."),
            jonas("Tschüss, bis morgen! [repeat]", "Bye, see you tomorrow!"),
            leo("«Tschüss» sounds friendly. What do I say to a stranger?"),
            maya("The formal goodbye. It goes with «Sie» and «Guten Tag». Picture a small shop where you've just paid. You'd say this to the shop owner."),
            jonas("Auf Wiedersehen, Herr Müller. [repeat]", "Goodbye, Mr Müller."),
            leo("Okay. And the little words? Please and thank you?"),
            maya("Thank you is «danke». Please is «bitte». And here's the surprise: «bitte» also means you're welcome. So someone holds a door, you say «danke», and they say «bitte»."),
            jonas("Danke. Bitte. [repeat]", "Thank you. You're welcome."),
            leo("And yes and no?"),
            maya("«Ja» and «nein». A polite no is no, thank you. And one long word you'll use every day means both excuse me and sorry. For example, asking where the toilet is."),
            jonas("Nein, danke. Entschuldigung, wo ist die Toilette? [repeat]", "No, thank you. Excuse me, where is the toilet?"),
            leo("[sigh] That word is so long. Can he say it in pieces?"),
            maya("He'll say it slowly. Think of it as four small beats, ent, shul, dee, goong, and then put them together."),
            jonas("Entschuldigung. [repeat]", "Excuse me; sorry."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("Three things. In the speaking part you start by introducing yourself to examiners you've never met, so use «Guten Tag» and «Sie». In the listening part, the greeting tells you if people are friends or strangers: «Hallo» and «Tschüss», or «Guten Tag» and «Auf Wiedersehen». And «bitte» is both please and you're welcome."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Greet four people in German, out loud, choosing «du» or «Sie» before you speak. «Guten Tag» to someone you don't know, and «Hallo» to someone you do. Say «bitte» and «danke» at least once, and end one of them with «Auf Wiedersehen». Every word from today is now in your «Wortschatz», your word deck."),
            jonas("Tschüss! Bis bald!", "Bye! See you soon!"),
            leo("See you soon, then. See you next time."),
        ),
        series = PodcastSeries.GERMAN_A1,
    )

    private val de1Grammar = Podcast(
        "de1-grammar",
        listOf(
            maya("Welcome back. Last time was hello and goodbye. Today is what sits underneath: the sounds German letters make, the alphabet for spelling your name, and the verb «sein», to be. Plus one rule about where the verb goes. And the good news first: German spelling is regular. Once you know the sounds, you can read any word aloud."),
            jonas("Hallo! Wie geht's?", "Hi! How are you?"),
            leo("«Mir geht es gut, danke.» See, I remember. But regular spelling? What's the catch?"),
            maya("A few letters make a different sound from English. The big one: the German letter w sounds like an English v. Water is «Wasser», and the question word how is «wie»."),
            jonas("Wasser. Wie. [repeat]", "Water. How."),
            leo("So where did the English w sound go?"),
            maya("German spelling doesn't have it. And the letter v moves over and sounds like f. So father is «Vater», and four is «vier». Picture the two letters swapping seats on a bus."),
            jonas("Vater. Vier. [repeat]", "Father. Four."),
            leo("Swapping seats. Got it. Any more troublemakers?"),
            maya("Two easy ones. The letter z is a sharp ts, as in ten and time. And j sounds like an English y, as in yes and year."),
            jonas("Zehn, Zeit. Ja, Jahr. [repeat]", "Ten. Time. Yes. Year."),
            leo("[sigh] And the vowel pairs? I never know which way round they go."),
            maya("Here's the trick: say the second letter's English name. E then I ends in I, so it sounds like eye. I then E ends in E, so it sounds like ee. No is «nein», like the number nine. And the polite you, «Sie», sounds like zee."),
            jonas("Nein. Mein. Sie. Wie. [repeat]", "No. My. You, polite. How."),
            leo("And the dots? I've seen dots on some letters."),
            maya("That's the «Umlaut», two dots that change the sound. Round your lips and say the vowel. You know one already from goodbye: «Tschüss». The letter that looks like a B is a sharp double s, as in street, «Straße». And C H is a soft hiss after E and I, and a sound in the throat after A, O and U."),
            jonas("Schön. Straße. Ich. Buch. [repeat]", "Beautiful. Street. I. Book."),
            leo("[chuckle] My mouth is tired. What's next?"),
            maya("Spelling. At any counter, and in the exam, someone will want your name letter by letter. This is the question you'll hear."),
            jonas("Wie schreibt man das? [repeat]", "How do you spell that?"),
            leo("And then I spell my name with English letters?"),
            maya("No, with the German letter names, and that's where names go wrong. German I is said ee, and E is said eh. A double letter is «Doppel». Here's how Usaid spells his name."),
            jonas("U, Es, A, I, De. Usaid. [repeat]", "U, S, A, I, D. Usaid."),
            leo("So the I says ee and the E says eh. Sneaky."),
            maya("Very. Now the verb «sein», to be. Changing a verb for the person is called «konjugieren». English does a little of it: I am, you are, she is. German does it for six persons. Start with I am."),
            jonas("Ich bin Usaid. [repeat]", "I am Usaid."),
            leo("And you are?"),
            maya("For a friend, «du bist». For he, she or it, «ist». Say Sanya is a teacher."),
            jonas("Du bist nett. Sie ist Lehrerin. [repeat]", "You are nice. She is a teacher."),
            leo("Wait, no a? She is teacher?"),
            maya("Right, German drops the a with jobs. And the ending of «Lehrerin» makes it a woman teacher. Now the plural: we, you all, and they. Usaid and Sanya together."),
            jonas("Wir sind aus Indien und Pakistan. [repeat]", "We are from India and Pakistan."),
            leo("And you all?"),
            maya("«ihr seid». Then «sie sind» for they, and the polite «Sie sind», which sounds the same. Here are all six in a row."),
            jonas("Ich bin, du bist, er ist, wir sind, ihr seid, sie sind. [repeat]", "I am, you are, he is, we are, you all are, they are."),
            leo("[laugh] «seid» and «sind» are out to get me."),
            maya("They are. Keep the pair apart: «ihr seid», «sie sind». And one trap: for I'm well, don't say «Ich bin gut». That means you're good at something. Say «Mir geht es gut». Now the biggest rule. In a plain statement, the verb comes second. Picture a train where the verb is always the second carriage."),
            jonas("Ich bin in Doha. Heute bin ich in Doha.", "I am in Doha. Today I am in Doha."),
            leo("Hang on. «Heute bin ich». In English I'd say today I am."),
            maya("That's the mistake to avoid. «Heute» takes the first seat, so the verb takes the second, and «ich» moves behind it. «Heute ich bin» is wrong: the verb would be third. Try it with tired."),
            jonas("Heute bin ich müde. [repeat]", "Today I am tired."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("Three things. In the speaking part, spell your name with the German letter names, and know the question «Wie schreibt man das?». In the reading part, pick the right form of «sein»: «wir sind», «ihr seid». And when you write, the verb is second in every sentence."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Ten minutes, out loud, with the recorder on. Read ten words with w, v, z and j. Spell your street name in German letters. Say «sein» for all six persons. Then say three sentences starting with «Heute», keeping the verb second."),
            jonas("Tschüss, bis morgen!", "Bye, see you tomorrow!"),
            leo("See you tomorrow, then. See you next time."),
        ),
        series = PodcastSeries.GERMAN_A1,
    )

    private val de1Dialogue = Podcast(
