            jonas("Guten Tag! Wie heißen Sie?", "Good day! What is your name?"),
            leo("That's the first question everywhere. Is a form just the same questions on paper?"),
            maya("Pretty much. A German form has boxes, and each box has a German name. The first two are your names. «Familienname» is your surname, the family name. «Vorname» is your first name, the one in front of it."),
            jonas("Familienname: Shahab. Vorname: Usaid. [repeat]", "Surname: Shahab. First name: Usaid."),
            leo("Surname first? I'd have written Usaid in the first box."),
            maya("Read the box, not your habit. Next comes «Land», the country. Then «Wohnort». That's the one that trips people up."),
            jonas("Land: Indien. Wohnort: Doha. [repeat]", "Country: India. Place of residence: Doha."),
            leo("«Wohnort». It sounds like a place."),
            maya("It is. «wohnen» is to live somewhere, and the rest of the word is the place. So it's the town you live in. The classic mistake is putting the town in «Land», or the country in «Wohnort». After that come «Alter», your age, and «Beruf», your job."),
            jonas("Alter: achtundzwanzig. Beruf: Softwareentwickler. [repeat]", "Age: twenty-eight. Job: software developer."),
            leo("And languages? Do I admit my German is only beginner level?"),
            maya("Yes. Writing the level beside each language is normal and honest. And here's the exam secret: in the form part, the situation already gives you the answers. You copy, you don't invent."),
            jonas("Sprachen: Englisch, Hindi, Deutsch. [repeat]", "Languages: English, Hindi, German."),
            leo("Okay, form done. What's the short message?"),
            maya("Four sentences to a new neighbour: name, country, town and job. Say Sanya has just met the woman next door. First, her name."),
            jonas("Hallo! Ich heiße Sanya. [repeat]", "Hello! My name is Sanya."),
            leo("Then where she's from?"),
            maya("With «aus». The country keeps no article, so it's just «aus» and the name of the country."),
            jonas("Ich komme aus Pakistan. [repeat]", "I come from Pakistan."),
            leo("And the town gets «in»?"),
            maya("Right. «aus» for where you come from, «in» for where you live. Those two little words carry the whole introduction. Sanya lives in Doha."),
            jonas("Ich wohne in Doha. [repeat]", "I live in Doha."),
            leo("And the job. «Ich bin eine Lehrerin»?"),
            maya("Nearly. Drop the «eine». German gives a job no article, so it's «Ich bin Lehrerin». You can add «von Beruf», by profession."),
            jonas("Ich bin Lehrerin von Beruf. [repeat]", "I am a teacher by profession."),
            leo("Four sentences. Is that really enough?"),
            maya("For this part, yes. And notice the verbs: «heiße», «komme», «wohne», «bin». Second in every sentence. Here's Usaid's whole message, with a friendly ending."),
            jonas("Hallo! Ich heiße Usaid Shahab. Ich komme aus Indien. Ich wohne in Doha. Ich bin Softwareentwickler von Beruf. Viele Grüße, Usaid.", "Hello! My name is Usaid Shahab. I come from India. I live in Doha. I am a software developer by profession. Best wishes, Usaid."),
            leo("«Viele Grüße». That's like best wishes?"),
            maya("Yes, a friendly way to end a message. Now say it out loud: thirty seconds for the speaking part. Name, where you're from, where you live, then your job and your languages, with «Ich spreche»."),
            jonas("Ich bin Softwareentwickler von Beruf. Ich spreche Englisch und Hindi. [repeat]", "I am a software developer by profession. I speak English and Hindi."),
