    private val de3Practice = Podcast(
        "de3-practice",
        listOf(
            maya("Welcome back. Today is practice: a week in Doha. We'll read a short text about Usaid's week, answer true or false questions like the reading part of the exam, and then write five sentences about a week of your own."),
            leo("Reading first. So I read the whole text, then the questions?"),
            maya("That's the mistake that eats your time. Scan first for the days and the clock times, because the questions are nearly always about when something happens. Here's the first sentence of the text."),
            jonas("Usaid arbeitet von Sonntag bis Donnerstag. [repeat]", "Usaid works from Sunday to Thursday."),
            leo("«Von» and «bis». From and to."),
            maya("Right. They frame a span, of days or of hours. Next comes his working day, in the official form, like a timetable. And listen for a new word, «fertig», which means finished."),
            jonas("Er beginnt um sieben Uhr und ist um fünfzehn Uhr dreißig fertig. [repeat]", "He starts at seven o'clock and is finished at fifteen thirty."),
            leo("Fifteen thirty. That's three thirty in the afternoon?"),
            maya("Yes, take twelve off. And in everyday German that's «halb vier», half-way to four. A question might use the other form on purpose, to test whether you can move between them. Then his study slot."),
            jonas("Um sechzehn Uhr fünfzehn lernt er eine Stunde Deutsch. [repeat]", "At sixteen fifteen he studies German for an hour."),
            leo("And his weekend?"),
            maya("Watch for one small word here. «Nicht» means not, and sitting in the middle of a sentence it flips the whole statement. Then a new verb, «einkaufen», to shop. It splits in two: «kauft» near the front, «ein» at the very end."),
            jonas("Am Freitag und am Samstag arbeitet er nicht. Am Samstag kauft er ein. [repeat]", "On Friday and on Saturday he does not work. On Saturday he goes shopping."),
            leo("[chuckle] The «ein» runs away to the end. And Sanya?"),
            maya("Sanya's week is the other pattern, Monday to Friday. And in the afternoon, the «Nachmittag», she has an English course."),
            jonas("Sanya arbeitet von Montag bis Freitag. Am Nachmittag hat sie einen Englischkurs. [repeat]", "Sanya works from Monday to Friday. In the afternoon she has an English course."),
            leo("Okay, quiz me."),
            maya("True or false, «richtig oder falsch». One: Usaid learns German at a quarter past four. The text said «sechzehn Uhr fünfzehn». Same moment, so «richtig». Two: Sanya works on Saturday. The text said Monday to Friday, so «falsch»."),
            leo("So really I just matched the times."),
            maya("Mostly. The third one is sneakier. Usaid is finished at half past three in the afternoon. The text says «fünfzehn Uhr dreißig», the question says «halb vier». The two forms name the same moment, so «richtig»."),
            leo("Sneaky. And now the writing?"),
            maya("Yes. Usaid writes his own week, with «ich». Work comes first, framed by «von» and «bis»."),
            jonas("Ich arbeite von Sonntag bis Donnerstag von sieben bis halb vier. [repeat]", "I work from Sunday to Thursday, from seven to half past three."),
            leo("And the study time?"),
            maya("«Um» for a clock time, and this time it goes first. Here's the rule that makes it sound German: when something else takes first place, the verb stays second, and «ich» moves in behind it."),
            jonas("Um Viertel nach vier lerne ich Deutsch. [repeat]", "At a quarter past four I study German."),
            leo("So not «Um Viertel nach vier ich lerne»?"),
            maya("No. Verb second, always. Then the day off and the weekend, both with «am» for the day."),
            jonas("Am Freitag arbeite ich nicht. Am Samstag kaufe ich ein. [repeat]", "On Friday I do not work. On Saturday I go shopping."),
            leo("That's four sentences. What's the fifth?"),
            maya("Something in the evening. Usaid calls Sanya. «Telefonieren», to phone, takes the same regular endings as «lernen»."),
            jonas("Am Abend telefoniere ich mit Sanya. [repeat]", "In the evening I phone Sanya."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("Three things. In the reading part, scan for days and times before the questions, and watch for «nicht». Be ready to move between the official clock and the everyday one: «fünfzehn Uhr dreißig» is «halb vier». And in the writing part, keep the verb second after «am Samstag» or «um acht»."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Say the time out loud every time you look at your phone today, at least five times. Use «halb» once, correctly. Say one in the official twenty-four hour form. And say your study slot, like «um Viertel nach vier»."),
            jonas("Bis Samstag! Tschüss!", "See you on Saturday! Bye!"),
            leo("Saturday it is. See you next time."),
        ),
        series = PodcastSeries.GERMAN_A1,
    )

    private val de4Words = Podcast(
        "de4-words",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is food and shopping: the words that get you through a supermarket, a bakery and a café. Bread, water, coffee, apples, and prices. And one habit for the whole episode: every food word comes with its little article, «der», «die» or «das». Jonas is here to say the German."),
            jonas("Hallo! Heute gehen wir einkaufen.", "Hello! Today we're going shopping."),
            leo("Good, because I'm hungry already. Where do we start?"),
            maya("At the bakery, with the most German food there is: bread. Bread is «das Brot». Say you're buying a loaf for breakfast."),
            jonas("Ich kaufe ein Brot. [repeat]", "I buy a loaf of bread."),
            leo("Why «ein» and not «eine»? I thought «ein» just meant a."),
            maya("It does mean a, but it follows the noun. «Brot» is «das», so it's «ein». And that's the habit: never learn a food word alone. Learn «das Brot», not just «Brot», like a first name and a surname. Half the work of a German noun is the article."),
            jonas("Der Kaffee. Der Tee. Die Milch. Das Wasser. [repeat]", "The coffee. The tea. The milk. The water."),
            leo("So coffee and tea are «der», milk is «die», water is «das». Is there a trick to guess them?"),
            maya("[chuckle] Not a reliable one, sorry. You learn the article with the word, every time. Now picture a café on a hot day. You sit down and order the simplest thing on the menu."),
            jonas("Ein Wasser, bitte. [repeat]", "A water, please."),
            leo("Now the supermarket. What goes in my trolley?"),
            maya("Rice is «der Reis». Chicken, as food, is «das Hähnchen». Vegetables are «das Gemüse», and fruit is «das Obst». And the apple, «der Apfel», changes when there's more than one. Listen to the vowel."),
            jonas("Der Apfel. Die Äpfel. [repeat]", "The apple. The apples."),
            leo("Okay. So how do I say two kilos of apples? What's the German word for of?"),
            maya("Here's the nice surprise: there isn't one. German is shorter than English here. The amount goes straight in front of the food. A kilo apples, a bottle water. Nothing in between."),
            jonas("Ein Kilo Äpfel, bitte. Eine Flasche Wasser. [repeat]", "A kilo of apples, please. A bottle of water."),
            leo("So I never put «von» in there?"),
            maya("Never. «Eine Flasche von Wasser» is the obvious guess from English, and Germans don't say it. Amount, then food. Now three verbs you'll use every day: to buy is «kaufen», to eat is «essen», and to drink is «trinken»."),
            jonas("Ich kaufe Brot und Milch. Ich trinke Kaffee. [repeat]", "I buy bread and milk. I drink coffee."),
            leo("And when I'm at the till and I want to know a price?"),
            maya("At the «Kasse», the checkout, you use «kosten», to cost. The cashier may also ask if you want a «Tüte», a bag, because bags cost a little. But the question every shop expects from you is this one."),
            jonas("Was kostet das? [repeat]", "What does that cost?"),
            leo("[sigh] And then they say a number really fast and I panic."),
            maya("Prices are easier than they sound. Euros first, then cents, with no word in between. And on a price tag in Germany the comma is the decimal point. So three comma fifty is three euros fifty."),
            jonas("Das kostet drei Euro fünfzig. [repeat]", "That costs three euros fifty."),
            leo("What about less than one euro? Or a special offer?"),
            maya("Under one euro, you just say the cents: eighty cents is «achtzig Cent». A special offer is an «Angebot», and the yellow signs love prices like this one."),
            jonas("Drei Euro neunundneunzig. Achtzig Cent. [repeat]", "Three euros ninety-nine. Eighty cents."),
            leo("Now how do I actually ask for things? Can I say «Ich will», like I want?"),
            maya("People will understand, but it sounds blunt, almost rude. The polite way is «Ich hätte gern», I would like. It works at the bakery counter, the market stall and the café."),
            jonas("Ich hätte gern ein Brot und eine Flasche Wasser. [repeat]", "I'd like a loaf of bread and a bottle of water."),
            leo("And when the food is good?"),
            maya("Then it's «lecker», delicious. You'll hear it everywhere. And at the end of a meal in a café, you ask for the bill, «die Rechnung»."),
            jonas("Das ist lecker! Die Rechnung, bitte. [repeat]", "That's delicious! The bill, please."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("Three things. Shopping talk is a favourite in the listening part, «Hören», so train your ear for prices: euros, then cents. In the speaking part you may need to ask for something, so «Ich hätte gern» and «Was kostet das?» And when you write, the amount goes straight before the food, with no «von»."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Write your real shopping list in German, ten items, with «der», «die» or «das» beside every noun. Then say three prices aloud, ask «Was kostet das?» out loud, and order something with «Ich hätte gern»."),
            jonas("Viel Spaß beim Einkaufen! Tschüss!", "Have fun shopping! Bye!"),
            leo("Happy shopping, everyone. See you next time."),
        ),
        series = PodcastSeries.GERMAN_A1,
    )

    private val de4Grammar = Podcast(
        "de4-grammar",
        listOf(
            maya("Welcome back. Today is grammar, and the big word is «Akkusativ», the accusative. It sounds scary, so here's the good news first: only one of the three genders actually changes. We'll also do plurals, and the two ways to say no, «nicht» and «kein»."),
            jonas("Hallo! Heute machen wir Grammatik.", "Hello! Today we're doing grammar."),
            leo("[sigh] Grammar. Okay, I'm ready. Where do we start?"),
            maya("With the «Artikel», the articles. Every German noun has a gender: masculine, feminine or neuter. You can't guess it reliably, so you learn the article as part of the word. Picture three drawers, labelled «der», «die» and «das»."),
            jonas("Der Apfel. Die Milch. Das Brot. [repeat]", "The apple. The milk. The bread."),
            leo("And the words for a? An apple, a bottle?"),
            maya("Each drawer has its own. «der» goes with «ein», «die» goes with «eine», and «das» goes with «ein» again. More than one thing, the plural, always takes «die», and it has no word for a at all."),
            jonas("Ein Apfel. Eine Flasche. Ein Brot. Die Äpfel. [repeat]", "An apple. A bottle. A loaf. The apples."),
            leo("Fine. Now the scary one. What is the accusative?"),
            maya("Picture a sentence as a little scene. Someone does something: that's the subject, the «Nominativ». Then there's the thing they buy, eat, see or have: that's the object, the «Akkusativ». In I eat the apple, I is the subject, and the apple is the object."),
            leo("And what happens to a noun when it becomes the object?"),
            maya("Only the masculine changes. «der» becomes «den», and «ein» becomes «einen». Feminine, neuter and plural stay exactly as they are. Listen to the apple as the subject, and then as the thing you eat."),
            jonas("Der Apfel ist rot. Ich esse den Apfel. [repeat]", "The apple is red. I eat the apple."),
            leo("So the apple itself didn't change. Just the little word in front."),
            maya("Exactly. Now with «ein». Coffee is «der Kaffee», masculine. So when you drink one, it's the object, and «ein» becomes «einen»."),
            jonas("Ich trinke einen Kaffee. [repeat]", "I drink a coffee."),
            leo("And milk? Does milk change too?"),
            maya("No. Milk is «die», feminine, so it stays. Bread is «das», neuter, so it stays too. Buy them, eat them, they look the same. Only the masculine moves."),
            jonas("Ich kaufe die Milch. Ich kaufe das Brot. [repeat]", "I buy the milk. I buy the bread."),
            leo("And tea? In a café I always want to say «der» there."),
            maya("Tea is «der Tee», masculine. But the tea you take is the object, so «der» becomes «den». And here's Sanya ordering her coffee in the same café."),
            jonas("Ich nehme den Tee. Sanya trinkt einen Kaffee. [repeat]", "I'll have the tea. Sanya drinks a coffee."),
            leo("Okay, I've got it. Now plurals. Is there one ending, like the English s?"),
            maya("[chuckle] I wish. There are five patterns. Many masculine words add an e: «der Tag», «die Tage». Most feminine words add n: «die Flasche», «die Flaschen». And masculine words ending in el, like «der Apfel», add nothing but take an umlaut."),
            jonas("Die Tage. Die Flaschen. Die Äpfel. [repeat]", "The days. The bottles. The apples."),
            leo("And the s, like English?"),
            maya("Only for words from other languages: «das Auto», «die Autos». Short neuter words often add er: «das Kind», «die Kinder». Now one small trap with verbs. A few common ones change their vowel for «du» and for «er». To eat is «essen», but you eat is «du isst»."),
            jonas("Du isst. Du sprichst. Du nimmst. Du fährst. [repeat]", "You eat. You speak. You take. You drive."),
            leo("So «du esst» is wrong?"),
            maya("Yes, that's a classic slip: it's «du isst». Now the last big idea, the two ways to say no. German has «nicht» and «kein», and they don't overlap. Ask yourself one question: am I saying no to a noun with «ein», or with no article at all? Then it's «kein»."),
            jonas("Ich habe keine Zeit. Ich habe kein Auto. [repeat]", "I have no time. I don't have a car."),
            leo("So «kein» is a no-word that sits exactly where «ein» would be?"),
            maya("That's the picture. And it takes the same endings as «ein», so it changes in the same one place: the masculine object. Brother is «der Bruder», so I have no brother is «keinen Bruder»."),
            jonas("Ich habe keinen Bruder. Ich trinke keinen Kaffee. [repeat]", "I have no brother. I don't drink coffee."),
            leo("And «nicht»? When does that one come in?"),
            maya("Everything else. A verb: I'm not working today. An adjective: the coffee isn't hot. And a noun that already has «der», «die», «das» or my in front of it, because then there's no «ein» for «kein» to replace."),
            jonas("Ich arbeite heute nicht. Der Kaffee ist nicht heiß. [repeat]", "I'm not working today. The coffee isn't hot."),
            leo("And where does «nicht» go? It kept moving around."),
            maya("When it says no to the whole sentence, it goes at the end. Before an adjective, it stands just in front of it. And when you say that's not my book, it sits right before the my."),
            jonas("Ich verstehe das nicht. Das ist nicht mein Buch. [repeat]", "I don't understand that. That's not my book."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("Three things. In reading and writing, «Lesen» and «Schreiben», check every masculine object: «einen», «den», «keinen». Feminine and neuter don't move, so leave them alone. In speaking, «Sprechen», say no to a noun with «kein», not «nicht». It's the commonest A1 mistake and examiners hear it at once. And learn every noun with its «Artikel»."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Prove to yourself that only the masculine moves. Say «der», «die», «das» and their accusative forms out loud, then do the same with «ein» and «kein». Make five sentences with a masculine object, like «einen Kaffee». Then say no to three of them, choosing «nicht» or «kein»."),
            jonas("Gut gemacht! Bis zum nächsten Mal.", "Well done! Until next time."),
            leo("Five sentences, coming up. See you next time."),
        ),
        series = PodcastSeries.GERMAN_A1,
    )

