    private val de4Practice = Podcast(
        "de4-practice",
        listOf(
            maya("Welcome back. Today is practice: reading a supermarket notice, and writing a short shopping note. The Goethe exam asks for both. The reading part shows adverts and notices, and the writing part asks for a short message. We'll do both with one shopping errand."),
            leo("An errand. So we're going to the supermarket again?"),
            maya("[chuckle] Again. First, the notice on the door of a Rewe supermarket. It starts with this week's special offers. One offer is an «Angebot», and more than one are «Angebote»."),
            jonas("Angebote diese Woche. Äpfel, ein Kilo: ein Euro neunundneunzig. Milch, ein Liter: neunundachtzig Cent.", "Offers this week. Apples, one kilo: one euro ninety-nine. Milk, one litre: eighty-nine cents."),
            leo("Wait, that's a lot of numbers. How do I read them in the exam?"),
            maya("One line at a time. On the notice they're written with a comma: one comma ninety-nine. The comma is the decimal point, so it's euros first, then cents. Under one euro, you just say the cents. Say the two prices after Jonas."),
            jonas("Ein Euro neunundneunzig. Neunundachtzig Cent. [repeat]", "One euro ninety-nine. Eighty-nine cents."),
            leo("What else is on the notice?"),
            maya("Bread, chicken and rice. Rice is two euros twenty a kilo. And the exam loves a little sum: how much do two kilos of rice cost?"),
            jonas("Zwei Kilo Reis kosten vier Euro vierzig. [repeat]", "Two kilos of rice cost four euros forty."),
            leo("[laugh] Maths in a language exam. Sneaky."),
            maya("Very gentle maths, though. Now the most important line on the notice: the opening hours. Listen for two words, «geöffnet», open, and «geschlossen», closed."),
            jonas("Geöffnet Montag bis Samstag von sieben bis zweiundzwanzig Uhr. Sonntag geschlossen. [repeat]", "Open Monday to Saturday from seven to ten p.m. Closed on Sunday."),
            leo("So it's open late on Saturday, but Sunday is out?"),
            maya("Exactly. Seven in the morning till ten at night, Monday to Saturday, so nine on a Saturday evening is fine. But «Sonntag geschlossen», closed on Sunday. A true or false question usually turns on the one day a notice leaves out, so always check it."),
            leo("Okay, I can read the notice. Now the note?"),
            maya("Now the note. Usaid is going shopping and writes to Sanya. The marker looks for four things: a greeting, the message, which is what and when, a question, and a sign-off. Picture four boxes on a sticky note. First the greeting, with a comma, and then when he's going."),
            jonas("Hallo Sanya, ich gehe um fünf Uhr in den Supermarkt. [repeat]", "Hi Sanya, I'm going to the supermarket at five o'clock."),
            leo("Why is «ich» small there? Isn't it the start of a sentence?"),
            maya("Good eye. The comma after the greeting doesn't end the sentence, so the next word stays lower case. And «um fünf Uhr» tells her when. Next, what he's buying. Watch the masculine object."),
            jonas("Ich kaufe einen Tee und ein Brot. [repeat]", "I'm buying a tea and a loaf of bread."),
            leo("«einen Tee», because tea is «der»?"),
            maya("Yes. The tea is the thing he buys, the object, so «ein» becomes «einen». «Brot» is «das», so it stays «ein». In a shopping note, the masculine is the one word worth checking. Then a question, so Sanya has something to answer."),
            jonas("Was möchtest du? [repeat]", "What would you like?"),
            leo("Is the question really that important?"),
            maya("It is. The exam asks for one and marks it. Skip it, and you lose a point before anyone reads your German. You can also ask what she needs, with «brauchen», to need."),
            jonas("Brauchst du Reis? [repeat]", "Do you need rice?"),
            leo("And the sign-off?"),
            maya("Short and friendly, because she's his partner: see you later, and his name."),
            jonas("Bis später, Usaid. [repeat]", "See you later, Usaid."),
            leo("Can we hear the whole note in one go?"),
            maya("Here's Usaid's note from start to finish. Count the four parts as you listen."),
            jonas("Hallo Sanya, ich gehe um fünf Uhr in den Supermarkt. Ich kaufe einen Tee und ein Brot. Was möchtest du? Bis später, Usaid.", "Hi Sanya, I'm going to the supermarket at five. I'm buying a tea and a loaf. What would you like? See you later, Usaid."),
            leo("Four parts, I counted. What does Sanya write back?"),
            maya("The same four parts. She's going to the market at seven, and buying an apple and a chicken. Apple is «der Apfel», so as the thing she buys, it changes too."),
            jonas("Hallo Usaid, ich gehe um sieben Uhr auf den Markt. Ich kaufe einen Apfel und ein Hähnchen. Brauchst du Reis? Bis später, Sanya.", "Hi Usaid, I'm going to the market at seven. I'm buying an apple and a chicken. Do you need rice? See you later, Sanya."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("Three things. In reading, «Lesen», notices turn on prices, hours and the word «geschlossen», so check the day a notice leaves out. In writing, «Schreiben», a note needs a greeting, the message, a question and a sign-off, and the question is marked. And check every masculine object: «einen Tee», not «ein Tee»."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Write your real shopping list in German, ten items with articles: «der Reis», «die Milch», «das Brot». Say the prices aloud, use «einen» for a masculine item, and order a tea in German, out loud."),
            jonas("Viel Erfolg! Bis später!", "Good luck! See you later!"),
            leo("Good luck with the list. See you next time."),
        ),
        series = PodcastSeries.GERMAN_A1,
    )

    private val de5Words = Podcast(
        "de5-words",
        listOf(
            maya("Welcome back. Today is the flat, the furniture and the city: where you live, what's in it, and what's around the corner. These are the words for two parts of the exam: flat adverts when you read, and directions when you listen."),
            jonas("Hallo! Ich wohne in Doha. Wir suchen eine Wohnung.", "Hello! I live in Doha. We are looking for a flat."),
            leo("So «Wohnung» is a flat, and «suchen» is looking for?"),
            maya("Yes. «die Wohnung», the flat. «suchen» is to look for, the part that takes months. When you finally get one, that's «finden», to find. And «mieten» is to rent it. Let's walk through a flat, room by room. Say a friend is showing you round. First the big picture."),
            jonas("Die Wohnung hat drei Zimmer. [repeat]", "The flat has three rooms."),
            leo("«Zimmer» is room. Which article does it take?"),
            maya("«das Zimmer». And here's a gift: most rooms are «das». The bedroom is the sleep room, «das Schlafzimmer». The living room is the live room, «das Wohnzimmer». And the bathroom is short and sweet: «das Bad»."),
            jonas("Das Schlafzimmer ist ruhig. Das Wohnzimmer ist hell. [repeat]", "The bedroom is quiet. The living room is bright."),
            leo("So every room is «das»? Please say yes."),
            maya("[chuckle] Almost. The kitchen is the one to watch: «die Küche». And outside there's «der Balkon», the balcony, and «der Garten», the garden. Picture the flat as mostly «das», with a feminine kitchen and two masculine things outdoors."),
            jonas("Die Küche ist groß und hell. [repeat]", "The kitchen is big and bright."),
            leo("Now the furniture. What goes in the rooms?"),
            maya("In the kitchen, «der Tisch», the table, and «der Stuhl», a chair. In the bedroom, «das Bett» and «der Schrank», the wardrobe. In the living room, «das Sofa» and «die Lampe». Let's start in the kitchen."),
            jonas("Der Tisch steht in der Küche. Wir brauchen vier Stühle. [repeat]", "The table is in the kitchen. We need four chairs."),
            leo("Four «Stühle»? Is that the plural of «Stuhl»?"),
            maya("It is: the vowel gets dots. Now the bedroom. Notice how «der», «die» or «das» rides along with every word. Learn them as one piece, like a first name and a family name."),
            jonas("Der Schrank ist im Schlafzimmer. Das Bett ist neu. [repeat]", "The wardrobe is in the bedroom. The bed is new."),
            leo("Okay, I've found a flat online. But the advert looks like a secret code."),
            maya("It reads in a fixed order: rooms, size, floor, rent, extras. «Zi.» is «Zimmer». «qm» is «Quadratmeter», square metres. «OG» is «Obergeschoss», an upper floor. And «NK» means «Nebenkosten», the bills. Jonas reads one out."),
            jonas("Drei Zimmer, fünfundsiebzig Quadratmeter, zweites Obergeschoss, sechshundertfünfzig Euro plus Nebenkosten.", "Three rooms, seventy-five square metres, second floor, six hundred and fifty euros plus bills."),
            leo("[sigh] Plus «Nebenkosten». So six hundred and fifty is not what I pay?"),
            maya("Right, that's trap number one: the bills come on top of the rent. Trap two: the ground floor is «Erdgeschoss», «EG». So the first «Obergeschoss» is one flight up. And trap three: «Zi.» counts the rooms you live in, not the kitchen or bathroom."),
            jonas("Die Miete ist sechshundert Euro. [repeat]", "The rent is six hundred euros."),
            leo("Adverts always sound lovely. Which words do they use?"),
            maya("The same handful, over and over. «hell», bright. «ruhig», quiet. «groß» and «klein», big and small. And «teuer» and «billig», expensive and cheap. No advert ever prints «teuer», of course."),
            jonas("Das Zimmer ist hell und ruhig, aber zu teuer. [repeat]", "The room is bright and quiet, but too expensive."),
            leo("[laugh] Story of my life. Now outside. How do I find the station?"),
            maya("City words first. «der Bahnhof», the station. «die Haltestelle», the bus or tram stop. «die Apotheke», the pharmacy. «die Post», the post office. Then three direction words: «links», left, «rechts», right, and «geradeaus», straight ahead. Say you're new in town and you stop someone."),
            jonas("Entschuldigung, wo ist der Bahnhof? [repeat]", "Excuse me, where is the station?"),
            leo("And what do I listen for in the answer?"),
            maya("A direction and a landmark. Maybe a corner, «die Ecke». In the listening part, those few words are the whole answer."),
            jonas("Die Post ist geradeaus. Die Apotheke ist um die Ecke. [repeat]", "The post office is straight ahead. The pharmacy is round the corner."),
            leo("And how do I ask if it's far?"),
            maya("«weit» is far, and «nah» is near. Short and handy."),
            jonas("Ist das weit? Nein, der Bahnhof ist nah. [repeat]", "Is it far? No, the station is near."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("Three things. In the reading part, flat adverts are shorthand: «Zi.», «qm», «OG», and plus «NK» means bills on top. In the listening part, directions come down to «links», «rechts», «geradeaus» and a landmark. And learn every room with its article. Most rooms are «das»."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Find three real flat adverts online and read only the abbreviations. Work out «Zi.», «OG» and «NK» in each, until you've worked out the code. Find the rent and whether it includes bills. Then name four things in your own room in German, with their articles. And the hallway? That's «der Flur»."),
            jonas("Tschüss! Bis bald!", "Bye! See you soon!"),
            leo("Good luck with the flat hunt. See you next time."),
        ),
        series = PodcastSeries.GERMAN_A1,
    )

    private val de5Grammar = Podcast(
        "de5-grammar",
        listOf(
            maya("Welcome back. Today is grammar, but two small, friendly tools: «es gibt», to say what's there, and the question «Wo?», where, which changes the little word in front of a noun. With these two you can describe a whole flat. Say you're telling a friend about your new street."),
            jonas("Es gibt einen Park. [repeat]", "There is a park."),
            leo("So «es gibt» is there is. But why «einen»? I learned «ein»."),
            maya("«es gibt» means there is or there are, and it never changes. The thing that exists is the object, so it goes into the accusative. And in the accusative only the masculine changes: «ein» becomes «einen». Feminine and neuter stay as they are. Listen."),
            jonas("Es gibt eine Küche. Es gibt ein Bad. [repeat]", "There is a kitchen. There is a bathroom."),
            leo("And more than one? Like three rooms?"),
            maya("Same two words. «es gibt» covers there are, too. In the plural there's no article at all, just the number."),
            jonas("Es gibt drei Zimmer. [repeat]", "There are three rooms."),
            leo("And if the flat has no balcony? [sigh] They never do."),
            maya("Then you use «kein», and it follows «ein» exactly. «der Balkon» is masculine, so «ein» becomes «einen», and «kein» becomes «keinen». Picture «es gibt» as a hand that grabs the noun: a masculine noun always comes out as «einen» or «keinen»."),
            jonas("Es gibt keinen Balkon, aber es gibt einen Garten. [repeat]", "There is no balcony, but there is a garden."),
            leo("Okay, I know what's in the flat. How do I say where things are?"),
            maya("That's tool two. Ask «Wo?», where. Pick a little position word: «in», «an», «auf», on, «neben», next to, «unter», under. Then the article after it switches to the «Dativ», the dative. Think of it as the article putting on a different coat when it answers where."),
            leo("A different coat. Which coats?"),
            maya("Three to learn. «der» and «das» both become «dem». «die» becomes «der». And the plural becomes «den», with an extra n on the noun. Let's start on the table. «der Tisch» becomes «dem Tisch»."),
            jonas("Die Lampe ist auf dem Tisch. [repeat]", "The lamp is on the table."),
            leo("And «die Küche»? Does that really become «der Küche»?"),
            maya("It does. It looks odd, but it's right. Where do we eat? In the kitchen. And it's not «in die Küche»; that answers a different question, which we'll meet in a minute."),
            jonas("Wir essen in der Küche. [repeat]", "We eat in the kitchen."),
            leo("And «das Bett»?"),
            maya("«das» becomes «dem», just like «der». So, where does the cat sleep?"),
            jonas("Die Katze schläft unter dem Bett. [repeat]", "The cat sleeps under the bed."),
            leo("[chuckle] Classic cat. And the plural, with the extra n?"),
            maya("«die Stühle», the chairs, becomes «den Stühlen». Don't forget that n at the end; it's an easy point to lose."),
            jonas("Die Kinder sitzen auf den Stühlen. [repeat]", "The children are sitting on the chairs."),
            leo("Wait. So I'd say «in dem Zentrum»? That's a mouthful."),
            maya("Nobody says that. Germans glue the two together, and that's called a «Kontraktion». «in dem» becomes «im», and «an dem» becomes «am». The exam writes them glued, so learn both: «im Zentrum», «am Bahnhof»."),
            jonas("Wir wohnen im Zentrum. Der Tisch steht am Fenster. [repeat]", "We live in the centre. The table stands by the window."),
            leo("You said «steht». The table stands?"),
            maya("Yes. German likes to say how things are placed. Upright things «stehen»: a table, a chair. Flat things «liegen», they lie: a book, a phone. And «neben», next to, takes the dative too."),
            jonas("Das Buch liegt auf dem Tisch. Die Bank ist neben der Post. [repeat]", "The book is lying on the table. The bank is next to the post office."),
            leo("And that other question you promised? Where to?"),
            maya("That's «Wohin?», where to. It's about movement, and it takes the accusative. At this level you only need to recognise it. Sitting in the cinema is «im Kino». Going to the cinema is «ins Kino»."),
            jonas("Ich bin im Kino. Ich gehe ins Kino.", "I'm at the cinema. I'm going to the cinema."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("Three things. In the reading part you'll see «im» and «am» everywhere, so read them as «in dem» and «an dem». When you write, «es gibt» takes «einen» with a masculine noun: «es gibt einen Park». And when you say where something is, «Wo?» means the dative: «dem», «der», «dem», «den»."),
            leo("Got it. And something to try today?"),
            maya("Walk round your home naming where things are. Say five sentences with «es gibt», then five with «in», «an» or «auf» plus the dative. Use «im» and «am» rather than «in dem» and «an dem». Then ask yourself «Wo ist das Sofa?», and answer it."),
            jonas("Bis zum nächsten Mal! Tschüss!", "Until next time! Bye!"),
            leo("«Tschüss», everyone. See you next time."),
        ),
        series = PodcastSeries.GERMAN_A1,
    )

    private val de5Dialogue = Podcast(
        "de5-dialogue",
        listOf(
            maya("Welcome back. Today is two short conversations, the kind you hear in the listening part of the exam. First, Usaid phones about a flat. Then Sanya asks the way to the station. In the exam each one is played twice, so we'll go piece by piece."),
            leo("A phone call in German. [sigh] My least favourite thing."),
            maya("It's more fixed than you think. The landlord, the «Vermieter», picks up and just says his name and hello. Here's Herr Weber."),
            jonas("Weber, guten Tag.", "Weber, good afternoon."),
            leo("Just his surname? No hello, this is?"),
            maya("That's normal in Germany: on the phone you answer with your family name. Now Usaid gives his name and says why he's calling. That second sentence is the one to steal. «wegen» means about: «wegen der Wohnung», about the flat."),
            jonas("Guten Tag, mein Name ist Shahab. Ich rufe wegen der Wohnung an. [repeat]", "Good afternoon, my name is Shahab. I'm calling about the flat."),
            leo("Wait. «rufe» near the start and «an» at the very end?"),
            maya("Yes. The verb is «anrufen», to phone, and it splits in two: «rufe» goes second, and «an» jumps to the end. Picture a sandwich, with the reason in the middle. Then the key question: is it still free?"),
            jonas("Ist die Wohnung noch frei? [repeat]", "Is the flat still free?"),
            leo("And what does Herr Weber say?"),
            maya("Yes, it's free, and then he lists the rooms. Listen for the number."),
            jonas("Ja, die Wohnung ist noch frei. Sie hat drei Zimmer, eine Küche und ein Bad.", "Yes, the flat is still free. It has three rooms, a kitchen and a bathroom."),
            leo("Can I ask about a balcony?"),
            maya("With «es gibt», there is, turned into a question. Balcony is «der Balkon», so it's «einen Balkon»."),
            jonas("Gibt es einen Balkon? [repeat]", "Is there a balcony?"),
            leo("Fingers crossed."),
            maya("No luck, but there's a small garden. And listen for the floor: «im zweiten Stock», the second floor. That's two flights up, and there may be no lift."),
            jonas("Nein, aber es gibt einen kleinen Garten. Die Wohnung ist im zweiten Stock.", "No, but there is a small garden. The flat is on the second floor."),
            leo("Okay, the big question: the rent. Do I say «Wie viel ist die Miete»?"),
            maya("Close, but in German rent is high or low. So you ask how high the rent is."),
            jonas("Wie hoch ist die Miete? [repeat]", "How much is the rent?"),
            leo("And the answer?"),
            maya("A number, and then the little word that matters most."),
            jonas("Sechshundertfünfzig Euro, plus Nebenkosten.", "Six hundred and fifty euros, plus bills."),
            leo("Plus bills. So I pay more than six hundred and fifty."),
            maya("Exactly. The «Nebenkosten», heating, water and rubbish, come on top of every figure. Last step: ask to see the flat, and suggest a day."),
            jonas("Kann ich die Wohnung sehen? Vielleicht am Samstag? [repeat]", "Can I see the flat? Perhaps on Saturday?"),
            leo("And he says yes?"),
            maya("Saturday at eleven, with the address. Catch the time and the street."),
            jonas("Ja, Samstag um elf Uhr. Die Adresse ist Gartenstraße zwölf.", "Yes, Saturday at eleven. The address is Gartenstraße twelve."),
            leo("Now the street. Is Sanya lost?"),
            maya("[chuckle] Just looking for the station. She stops a «Passant», a passer-by. One fixed opening, one fixed question."),
            jonas("Entschuldigung, wo ist der Bahnhof? [repeat]", "Excuse me, where is the station?"),
            leo("And this is where I panic and understand nothing."),
            maya("Then listen only for direction words: «geradeaus», straight on, «links», left, «rechts», right. And «die zweite Straße», the second street."),
            jonas("Gehen Sie hier geradeaus und dann die zweite Straße links. [repeat]", "Go straight on here and then the second street on the left."),
            leo("Straight on, second left. And how do I know I'm there?"),
            maya("A landmark: next to the post office. «neben» answers where, so it takes the dative, and «die Post» becomes «der Post»."),
            jonas("Der Bahnhof ist neben der Post. [repeat]", "The station is next to the post office."),
            leo("And is it far?"),
            maya("Sanya asks exactly that. The answer is about five minutes «zu Fuß», on foot."),
            jonas("Ist das weit? Nein, etwa fünf Minuten zu Fuß. [repeat]", "Is it far? No, about five minutes on foot."),
            leo("And to say thank you?"),
            maya("«Vielen Dank», thank you very much. And the passer-by answers «Gern geschehen», you're welcome."),
            jonas("Vielen Dank! Gern geschehen.", "Thank you very much! You're welcome."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("Three things. In the listening part, hear the rent and then the plus: «plus Nebenkosten» means you pay more. Catch the floor, «im zweiten Stock». For directions, listen for «geradeaus», «links», «rechts» and a landmark. And in the speaking part, it's «Wie hoch ist die Miete?», not «wie viel»."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Phone about a flat, out loud. Say why you're calling in one sentence, with «wegen der Wohnung». Ask how many rooms. Ask «Wie hoch ist die Miete?» Then ask to see it, and propose a day, like «vielleicht am Samstag»."),
            jonas("Tschüss, bis bald!", "Bye, see you soon!"),
            leo("Good luck with the phone call. See you next time."),
        ),
        series = PodcastSeries.GERMAN_A1,
