    private val de6Grammar = Podcast(
        "de6-grammar",
        listOf(
            maya("Welcome back. Today is grammar, and it's the most useful grammar in A1: three small verbs. «können», can. «müssen», must. «möchten», would like. With them you can say what you can do, what you have to do and what you'd like to do. And there's one trick: where the second verb goes."),
            jonas("Ich kann schwimmen. [repeat]", "I can swim."),
            leo("Easy enough. «kann» is can. So what's the trick?"),
            maya("Grammar books call these verbs «Modalverben», modal verbs. A modal hardly ever works alone. It brings a second verb, and that second verb goes to the very end, in its dictionary form, the «Infinitiv». Listen to Usaid on a busy day at the office."),
            jonas("Ich muss heute lange arbeiten. [repeat]", "I have to work late today."),
            leo("So «muss» is second, and «arbeiten» is all the way at the back."),
            maya("Exactly. Picture two bookends on a shelf. The modal is the left bookend, in second place. The infinitive is the right bookend, at the end. Everything else, «heute», «lange», sits on the shelf between them. German calls this «die Satzklammer», the sentence bracket."),
            leo("Can't I just say «Ich muss arbeiten heute», like in English?"),
            maya("That's the mistake everyone makes. «heute» goes inside the bracket, and «arbeiten» stays last. And the infinitive never changes. Only the modal changes to match the person. Here's Sanya, who starts school very early."),
            jonas("Sanya muss um sieben aufstehen. [repeat]", "Sanya has to get up at seven."),
            leo("Okay. So how does the modal change? «ich kann», «du»... what?"),
            maya("Here's the good news. For «ich», and for «er» or «sie», the form is the same, with no ending at all: «ich kann», «er kann». «du» adds an «st». And «können» drops its umlaut for «ich», «du» and «er»."),
            jonas("Ich kann. Du kannst. Er kann. Wir können. [repeat]", "I can. You can. He can. We can."),
            leo("And «müssen» and «möchten»?"),
            maya("Same pattern. «müssen» drops its umlaut too: «ich muss», «du musst», «wir müssen». And «ich möchte», «du möchtest», «wir möchten». Now a question. You ask a colleague for help. The modal jumps to the front, and the infinitive still waits at the end."),
            jonas("Kannst du mir helfen? [repeat]", "Can you help me?"),
            leo("And if I can't come to something? Where does «nicht» go?"),
            maya("Inside the bracket, just before the infinitive. Say your boss invites you to a meeting, and today you can't make it."),
            jonas("Ich kann heute nicht kommen. [repeat]", "I can't come today."),
            leo("What about «möchten»? That sounds like a café word."),
            maya("It is. «möchten» is the polite wish, would like. With a second verb, the bracket works as usual. But if you only want a thing, you don't need a second verb at all."),
            jonas("Ich möchte einen Kaffee trinken. Ich möchte einen Kaffee. [repeat]", "I'd like to drink a coffee. I'd like a coffee."),
            leo("[laugh] Both ways I get coffee. Are there more of these verbs?"),
            maya("Two more. «wollen» is a strong want, and «dürfen» is permission, may. Picture planning a trip, and then a sign on the wall saying no smoking here."),
            jonas("Wir wollen nach Deutschland fahren. Hier darf man nicht rauchen. [repeat]", "We want to travel to Germany. You may not smoke here."),
            leo("There was another bit in this topic. My boss, your office?"),
            maya("Yes, the «Possessivartikel», the words for my, your, his. They behave exactly like «ein»: «mein Chef», «meine Firma», «mein Büro». And a masculine noun after the verb takes «en»: «meinen Chef»."),
            jonas("Mein Bruder ist Arzt. Ich frage meinen Chef. [repeat]", "My brother is a doctor. I'll ask my boss."),
            leo("And his, her, our?"),
            maya("«sein» is his, «ihr» is her, «unser» is our. Careful with the polite one: «Ihr» with a capital letter means your, for «Sie». So to a stranger, you ask for a phone number like this."),
            jonas("Sein Büro ist groß. Wie ist Ihre Telefonnummer? [repeat]", "His office is big. What is your phone number?"),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("Three things. In the writing part, check every sentence with a modal: modal second, infinitive last and unchanged. In the speaking part, «Kannst du mir helfen?» and «Ich möchte» are your polite requests. And when you read, «Ihre» with a capital letter is the formal your."),
            leo("And what do I practise today?"),
            maya("Ten minutes. Write five sentences with «können», five with «müssen», and three with «möchten». Then go through them and check that the second verb is last and unchanged in every one. Read them out loud."),
            jonas("Viel Spaß! Bis zum nächsten Mal!", "Have fun! Until next time!"),
            leo("I have to go. Or rather, «ich muss gehen». See you next time."),
        ),
        series = PodcastSeries.GERMAN_A1,
    )

