    private val de7Grammar = Podcast(
        "de7-grammar",
        listOf(
            maya("Welcome back. Today is the grammar of travel: verbs that split in two, and three little words that say where to and how, «nach», «zu» and «mit». Get these, and every announcement at the station starts to make sense."),
            leo("Verbs that split in two? That sounds painful."),
            maya("[chuckle] It's easier than it sounds. German calls it a «trennbares Verb», a separable verb. Take «abfahren», to depart. The little front piece, the «Präfix», is «ab». Picture a train: the engine at the front, and the last carriage at the very end. In a sentence, the verb sits in second place, and the prefix waits at the end."),
            jonas("Der Zug fährt um neun Uhr ab. [repeat]", "The train departs at nine."),
            leo("So «fährt» is second, and «ab» is the very last word? Even with other stuff in between?"),
            maya("Even then. Everything else goes in the middle: the time, the place. The same with «ankommen», to arrive. Say Usaid and Sanya are travelling to Hamburg together."),
            jonas("Wir kommen um zwölf Uhr an. [repeat]", "We arrive at twelve."),
            leo("And changing trains? «umsteigen»?"),
            maya("Same pattern. «steige» in second place, «um» at the end. Say you change trains in Cologne."),
            jonas("Ich steige in Köln um. [repeat]", "I change trains in Cologne."),
            leo("Is it only travel verbs that do this?"),
            maya("No, they're all over your day. «aufstehen», to get up. «einkaufen», to shop. «mitkommen», to come along. «fernsehen», to watch TV. The common prefixes are «ab», «an», «auf», «aus», «ein», «mit», «um» and «zurück». Here's your morning, and an invitation."),
            jonas("Ich stehe um sechs auf. Kommst du mit? [repeat]", "I get up at six. Are you coming along?"),
            leo("Does the verb ever stay in one piece?"),
            maya("Yes, and that's the second half of the rule. With a helper verb like «müssen», must, or «möchten», would like, the helper takes second place, and the whole verb goes to the end, closed up again. The train is coupled back together."),
            jonas("Ich muss in Köln umsteigen. Ich muss um sechs Uhr aufstehen. [repeat]", "I have to change in Cologne. I have to get up at six."),
            leo("And how do I say these? Where does the stress go?"),
            maya("On the prefix, always: «ankommen», «umsteigen», «abfahren». One warning: «be», «er» and «ver» are not separable, they stay put. «Ich verstehe» never splits. Here's the arrival question."),
            jonas("Ankommen. Wann kommst du an? [repeat]", "To arrive. When are you arriving?"),
            leo("Okay. Now the where-to words. How do I say I'm going to Berlin?"),
            maya("The question is «Wohin?», where to? For cities and most countries, it's «nach», with no article at all. And going home is «nach Hause»."),
            jonas("Wir fahren nach Berlin. Ich gehe nach Hause. [repeat]", "We're going to Berlin. I'm going home."),
            leo("So I can say I'm going to the doctor with «nach»?"),
            maya("That's the classic mistake. For people, and places with an article, use «zu». And «zu» joins with the article: «zu dem» becomes «zum», «zu der» becomes «zur». «Der Arzt» gives «zum Arzt», «der Bahnhof» gives «zum Bahnhof», «die Schule» gives «zur Schule»."),
            jonas("Ich gehe zum Arzt. Sanya geht zur Schule. [repeat]", "I'm going to the doctor. Sanya goes to school."),
            leo("And going into a building? Like the cinema?"),
            maya("Then it's «in», and «in das» shrinks to «ins»: «ins Kino». Now the last word: how you travel. That's «mit», by, and «mit» always changes the article. «Der» and «das» become «dem», and «die» becomes «der»."),
            jonas("Sanya fährt mit dem Zug. Ich fahre mit der U-Bahn. [repeat]", "Sanya goes by train. I take the underground."),
            leo("[sigh] «mit der», even though it's «die U-Bahn». Really?"),
            maya("Really. Think of «mit» as a little machine that reshapes the article. And it's never «mit den Zug». Now let's put it all together: where to, how, and a split verb."),
            jonas("Wir fahren mit dem Zug nach Hamburg und steigen in Hannover um. [repeat]", "We go to Hamburg by train and change in Hanover."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("Three things. In «Hören», the prefix comes last, so wait for the end of the sentence: «fährt», then «ab», means departs. In «Lesen», «nach» marks the town a train is going to. And in «Schreiben», write «mit dem Zug», never «mit den»."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Ten minutes. Say five sentences with «abfahren», «ankommen» and «umsteigen». Then say each again with «müssen», so the verb stays whole at the end. Use «nach», «zu» and «mit» once each, and stress the prefix: «ankommen»."),
            jonas("Tschüss! Bis bald!", "Bye! See you soon!"),
            leo("Bye for now. See you next time."),
        ),
        series = PodcastSeries.GERMAN_A1,
    )

