    private val de9Grammar = Podcast(
        "de9-grammar",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is grammar, in three small pieces: how to say you like something, where time words go in a sentence, and a first look at the past. Each piece is short, I promise."),
            leo("[sigh] Grammar. Okay, start with the easy one."),
            maya("Liking. You already know «gern»: it goes right after a verb, and it means you like doing that. Picture Usaid at the pool on a Saturday."),
            jonas("Ich schwimme gern. [repeat]", "I like swimming."),
            leo("But what if I like a thing, not an activity? Like tea?"),
            maya("Then you need a different word: «mögen», to like. «gern» likes doing something; «mögen» likes a thing or a person. With «ich» it becomes «ich mag»."),
            jonas("Ich mag Tee. Ich mag meine Kollegen. [repeat]", "I like tea. I like my colleagues."),
            leo("So can I say «Ich mag schwimmen»?"),
            maya("That's the mistake to avoid. «mögen» goes with nouns. Swimming is something you do, so it's «Ich schwimme gern». But football as a thing, the sport itself? That's a noun, so «mögen» works."),
            jonas("Ich mag Fußball. Ich spiele gern Fußball. [repeat]", "I like football. I like playing football."),
            leo("And «möchten»? It sounds a lot like «mögen»."),
            maya("It's a close cousin, but it means wanting, right now. At the café you don't say you like tea in general. You say you'd like one now."),
            jonas("Ich möchte einen Tee. [repeat]", "I'd like a tea."),
            leo("And if I don't like something?"),
            maya("For an activity, «nicht gern». For a thing, «mögen» with «kein». Fish is «der Fisch», so you say «keinen Fisch»."),
            jonas("Ich tanze nicht gern. Ich mag keinen Fisch. [repeat]", "I don't like dancing. I don't like fish."),
            leo("Okay, piece two. Where do the time words go?"),
            maya("A time word is a «Zeitangabe»: «heute», «morgen», «am Samstag», «im Sommer». Picture a sentence as a row of seats. The verb has a reserved seat, always number two. Start with «ich», and the time word sits after the verb."),
            jonas("Ich spiele am Samstag Fußball. [repeat]", "I play football on Saturday."),
            leo("And if I want to start with Saturday?"),
            maya("You can. But only one thing fits in front of the verb. Saturday takes seat one, the verb keeps seat two, so «ich» has to move behind the verb."),
            jonas("Am Samstag spiele ich Fußball. [repeat]", "On Saturday I play football."),
            leo("So «Am Samstag ich spiele» is wrong?"),
            maya("Wrong, and it's the most common mistake at A1: two things in front of the verb. English does that, German doesn't. The same goes for «heute», today, or a season."),
            jonas("Heute gehen wir ins Kino. Im Herbst regnet es oft. [repeat]", "Today we're going to the cinema. In autumn it often rains."),
            leo("What if I have when, how and where, all in one sentence?"),
            maya("Then the order is time, manner, place. When, how, where. Say you're taking the bus to «Köln» on Sunday. English usually puts the place first, so this is the order to unlearn."),
            jonas("Ich fahre am Sonntag mit dem Bus nach Köln. [repeat]", "I'm going to Cologne by bus on Sunday."),
            leo("And words for how often? Always, never?"),
            maya("That's «Häufigkeit», frequency: «immer», «oft», «manchmal», «nie». Always, often, sometimes, never. They sit right after the verb, like a time word. And «manchmal» can open the sentence too."),
            jonas("Wir gehen oft spazieren. Manchmal koche ich. [repeat]", "We often go for a walk. Sometimes I cook."),
            leo("And piece three, the past. Is it scary?"),
            maya("Not today. German talks about the past with the «Perfekt». Think of two hands holding the sentence. «haben» sits in seat two, and a special form called the «Partizip» goes to the very end."),
            jonas("Ich habe gestern Fußball gespielt. [repeat]", "Yesterday I played football."),
            leo("How do I build that «gespielt» word?"),
            maya("Most verbs follow one recipe: «ge», then the stem, then a «t». «spielen» becomes «gespielt», «kochen» becomes «gekocht», «hören» becomes «gehört». «arbeiten» adds a little extra: «gearbeitet»."),
            jonas("Sanya hat Reis gekocht. Ich habe Musik gehört. [repeat]", "Sanya cooked rice. I listened to music."),
            leo("[chuckle] And how do I ask a friend what they did?"),
            maya("«machen» becomes «gemacht». Picture Monday morning at the language school, asking about the weekend. Notice the «Partizip» still goes last, after the time."),
            jonas("Was hast du am Wochenende gemacht? [repeat]", "What did you do at the weekend?"),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("Three things. In «Sprechen» and «Schreiben», keep the verb second, even after «am Samstag» or «heute». Use «gern» with a verb and «mögen» with a thing. And in «Hören», a story about the past has «haben» early and the «Partizip» at the end, so wait for the last word."),
            leo("Nice. And what do I try today?"),
            maya("Write five sentences starting with a time phrase, and check the verb is second in all five. Use «gern», «lieber» and «am liebsten» once each. Then say two of them again in the «Perfekt». Ten minutes, out loud."),
            jonas("Bis bald! Schönes Wochenende!", "See you soon! Have a nice weekend!"),
            leo("Have a great weekend, everyone. See you next time."),
        ),
        series = PodcastSeries.GERMAN_A1,
    )

