            leo("Okay. Now already and not yet. My mum always asks if I've eaten."),
            maya("[chuckle] Then you need these two. «Schon» is already, «noch nicht» is not yet. And a handy verb: «frühstücken», to have breakfast. Its past form is «gefrühstückt»."),
            jonas("Ich habe schon gegessen. Ich habe noch nicht gefrühstückt. [repeat]", "I have already eaten. I haven't had breakfast yet."),
            leo("How do I tell a whole day? First this, then that?"),
            maya("Three little words put a day in order: «zuerst», first, «dann», then, and «danach», after that. And «am Abend», in the evening, to finish. Picture an ordinary workday."),
            jonas("Zuerst bin ich aufgestanden. Dann habe ich gefrühstückt. [repeat]", "First I got up. Then I had breakfast."),
            leo("Hold on. «bin ich aufgestanden», but «habe ich gefrühstückt». Why two different ones?"),
            maya("That's the rule to learn with every verb. A past sentence needs a helper, «haben» or «sein». Most verbs take «haben». But verbs that move you from one place to another take «sein». Getting up is a change too, so «sein». Think of «sein» as the suitcase verbs."),
            jonas("Danach bin ich zur Arbeit gefahren. Am Abend habe ich gekocht. [repeat]", "After that I drove to work. In the evening I cooked."),
