    private val de10Grammar = Podcast(
        "de10-grammar",
        listOf(
            maya("Welcome back. Today is grammar: how German talks about the past. Spoken German mostly uses one past form, the «Perfekt», and it's built like a sandwich: a helper verb near the front, and the past form of the main verb at the very end."),
            leo("A sandwich. I like it already. What's the helper?"),
            maya("The «Hilfsverb», the helper, is either «haben» or «sein». It sits in the usual verb place, second. The past form, called the participle, goes to the end. Here's a question you'll hear every Monday."),
            jonas("Was hast du gestern gemacht? [repeat]", "What did you do yesterday?"),
            leo("So «hast» is the helper, and «gemacht» waits at the end."),
            maya("Exactly. And the answer has the same shape: the helper second, everything else in the middle, the participle last."),
            jonas("Ich habe Pizza gegessen. [repeat]", "I ate pizza."),
            leo("Could I say «Ich habe gegessen Pizza», like in English?"),
            maya("That's the classic mistake. In English, eaten sits next to have. In German, the participle waits at the end, after the pizza. Now the big question: «haben» or «sein»?"),
            leo("Yes, how do I choose?"),
            maya("Picture a trip from A to B. If the verb moves you from one place to another, or changes your state, like getting up, it takes «sein». Everything else takes «haben»: eating, buying, reading, working. Going to the cinema is A to B."),
            jonas("Ich bin ins Kino gegangen. [repeat]", "I went to the cinema."),
            leo("And driving? Flying?"),
            maya("Same picture. Usaid and Sanya drive to Cologne, and Sanya flies to Doha. Both are A to B, so both take «sein»."),
            jonas("Wir sind nach Köln gefahren. Sanya ist nach Doha geflogen. [repeat]", "We went to Cologne. Sanya flew to Doha."),
            leo("Any verbs that break the picture?"),
            maya("Two, and the exam likes both. «bleiben», to stay, and «passieren», to happen. Nothing moves, but they take «sein» anyway. Learn them as exceptions."),
            jonas("Wir sind zu Hause geblieben. Was ist passiert? [repeat]", "We stayed at home. What happened?"),
            leo("[sigh] Okay. And how do I build the participle itself?"),
            maya("There are three kinds, plus some with no «ge». Regular verbs: «ge», the stem, then «t». «machen», «gemacht». «kaufen», «gekauft». Irregular verbs: «ge», often a changed stem, then «en». «trinken», «getrunken». «schreiben», «geschrieben»."),
            jonas("Ich habe Brot gekauft. Ich habe eine E-Mail geschrieben. [repeat]", "I bought bread. I wrote an email."),
            leo("And the verbs with a little front piece, like «einkaufen»?"),
            maya("Those are separable. The «ge» slips inside, after the front piece: «eingekauft», «aufgestanden». And verbs starting with «be», «ver» or «er», or ending in «ieren», take no «ge» at all: «besucht», «verstanden», «telefoniert»."),
            jonas("Sanya hat ihre Eltern besucht. Ich habe alles verstanden. [repeat]", "Sanya visited her parents. I understood everything."),
            leo("Great. So how do I say I was ill? «Ich bin krank gewesen»?"),
            maya("[chuckle] Correct, but nobody says it. For was and had, German uses the simple past, the «Präteritum»: «war» and «hatte». At A1, those are the only two you need."),
            jonas("Gestern war ich krank. Wir hatten keine Zeit. [repeat]", "Yesterday I was ill. We had no time."),
            leo("Do they change with the person?"),
            maya("A little. «ich war», «du warst», «er war», «wir waren», «ihr wart», «sie waren». And «hatte» goes the same way: «hatte», «hattest», «hatte», «hatten», «hattet», «hatten». Notice that «ich» and «er» look the same."),
            jonas("Ich war, du warst, wir waren. Ich hatte, du hattest, wir hatten. [repeat]", "I was, you were, we were. I had, you had, we had."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("Three things. In Hören and Lesen, the helper and the participle are far apart, so wait for the end of the sentence before you decide what happened. In Sprechen and Schreiben, movement takes «sein», and so do «bleiben» and «passieren». And for was and had, use «war» and «hatte», not the «Perfekt»."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Twenty participles, out loud, with the right helper in front of each. Ten with «haben», five with «sein». Then say «war» and «hatte» for all six persons. And put the participle last every time."),
            jonas("Das war's für heute. Tschüss!", "That's it for today. Bye!"),
            leo("Bye! See you next time."),
        ),
        series = PodcastSeries.GERMAN_A1,
    )

