            maya("[chuckle] It's copying, with a few traps. The traps are the field names, so we learn them like vocabulary. First, the instruction you'll see on top of almost every form."),
            jonas("Füllen Sie das Formular aus. [repeat]", "Fill in the form."),
            leo("«Formular» is the form. Is it der, die or das?"),
            maya("It's «das Formular». And the verb «ausfüllen», to fill in, splits in two: «füllen» near the front, «aus» at the very end. Now picture Sanya at the language school, signing up for a German course. The first two fields are her surname and her first name."),
            jonas("Familienname: Khan. Vorname: Sanya. [repeat]", "Surname: Khan. First name: Sanya."),
            leo("Wait. «Familienname» isn't the size of my family?"),
            maya("[laugh] No, it's the family name, the surname. «Vorname» is the name that comes before, the first name. Both are «der». Swapping them is the classic mistake. Next pair: Sanya was born in Lahore, and she lives in Köln."),
            jonas("Geburtsort: Lahore. Wohnort: Köln. [repeat]", "Place of birth: Lahore. Place of residence: Cologne."),
            leo("They both end in «Ort». How do I keep them apart?"),
            maya("«Ort» means place. «Geburt» is birth, so «Geburtsort» is where you were born. «Wohnen» is to live, so «Wohnort» is where you live now. The exam puts them side by side on purpose. Now the date of birth. Sanya was born on the fifth of May, nineteen ninety-eight. Said out loud in German, that's this."),
            jonas("Der fünfte Mai neunzehnhundertachtundneunzig. [repeat]", "The fifth of May, nineteen ninety-eight."),
            leo("And how do I write it on the form?"),
            maya("Day, then month, then year, with full stops: zero five, dot, zero five, dot, nineteen ninety-eight. It's the one field you don't copy word for word. The text says it in words; the form wants digits. When you say a date, you use the ordinal: first, second, third. Most just add «te»."),
            jonas("Der erste, der zweite, der dritte, der siebte. [repeat]", "The first, the second, the third, the seventh."),
            leo("Those don't all follow the rule, do they?"),
            maya("No. «Erste», «dritte» and «siebte» are the odd ones, so learn them as they are. The rest add «te» up to nineteen, like «der zwölfte», and «ste» from twenty, like «der zwanzigste». Now nationality. Sanya is from Pakistan. What goes in «Staatsangehörigkeit»?"),
            jonas("Staatsangehörigkeit: pakistanisch. [repeat]", "Nationality: Pakistani."),
            leo("Not «Pakistan»? That's where she's from."),
            maya("That's the country. The field wants the adjective, with a small letter: «pakistanisch», «deutsch», «indisch», «katarisch». Then the address: «Straße» and «Hausnummer», street and house number, and the postcode, «die Postleitzahl», «PLZ» for short, with the town."),
            jonas("Schulstraße acht, fünf null sechs sechs sieben Köln. [repeat]", "8 School Street, 50667 Cologne."),
            leo("And the forms with little squares? I've seen those."),
            maya("Those say «bitte ankreuzen», please tick. Sanya wants the evening course, so she puts a cross by «abends», not «vormittags». If you write an answer beside an empty box, it scores nothing."),
            jonas("Vormittags oder abends? Bitte ankreuzen. [repeat]", "Mornings or evenings? Please tick."),
            leo("Is it always a language course, or could it be something else?"),
            maya("Often it's a hotel or a trip. Then you see «Anzahl», number of, and «Nacht» or «Nächte». «Zahlung» is payment: in cash, or «Kreditkarte». And sometimes «Familienstand», marital status: «ledig» or «verheiratet»."),
            jonas("Ein Doppelzimmer für zwei Nächte. Ich zahle mit Kreditkarte. [repeat]", "A double room for two nights. I pay by credit card."),
            leo("And the very bottom of the form?"),
            maya("«Datum» and «Unterschrift», date and signature. And never leave a gap blank. Every gap carries a point, so a guess from the text is better than nothing."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("Three things for «Schreiben», part one. The field names are the whole task, so learn them as words. Every answer is in the situation: copy carefully, «Geburtsort» not «Wohnort», and invent nothing. And two formats: dates as day, month, year with dots, and nationality as an adjective."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Find a real German form online and fill it in with your own details. Write your «Geburtsdatum» as day, month, year with dots. Write your «Staatsangehörigkeit» as an adjective. On a choice field, tick rather than write. Then say the whole date out loud as an ordinal, like Jonas did."),
            jonas("Viel Erfolg beim Formular! Tschüss!", "Good luck with the form! Bye!"),
            leo("Good luck, everyone. See you next time."),
        ),
        series = PodcastSeries.GERMAN_A1,
    )

