            jonas("Sehr geehrte Frau Braun. Sehr geehrter Herr Weber. [repeat]", "Dear Ms Braun. Dear Mr Weber."),
            leo("[chuckle] «Sehr geehrte» sounds very grand for a note about a missed class."),
            maya("It's just the polite dear. Now a small trap. After the greeting comes a comma, and the next line starts with a small letter, because the sentence carries on. A new line is not a new sentence."),
            jonas("Liebe Lena, ich komme gern. [repeat]", "Dear Lena, I'd love to come."),
            leo("Okay. And at the bottom?"),
            maya("The sign-off has to match the greeting. With «du»: «Viele Grüße», «Liebe Grüße», or «Bis bald». With «Sie»: «Mit freundlichen Grüßen». No full stop after it, and your name goes on the next line."),
            jonas("Viele Grüße, Usaid. Mit freundlichen Grüßen, Usaid Shahab. [repeat]", "Best wishes, Usaid. Yours sincerely, Usaid Shahab."),
            leo("What if I mix them? «Sehr geehrte» at the top and «Viele Grüße» at the bottom?"),
            maya("Then you lose the mark for register, however good your German is. Now the middle: three content points, the «Inhaltspunkte». Each one is marked on its own. Cover two beautifully and skip one, and that one's marks are gone."),
            leo("So how do I make sure I hit all three?"),
            maya("One line per point, in the task's order. Here's a real task. You can't come to your German class on Saturday. Write to Frau Braun: why not, what you do at home, and when you come back. Point one, why not."),
            jonas("Ich kann am Samstag leider nicht zum Kurs kommen, weil ich arbeiten muss. [repeat]", "Unfortunately I can't come to class on Saturday, because I have to work."),
            leo("Does «weil» send the verb right to the end?"),
            maya("Yes, «muss» goes last. And a reason with «weil» is also an easy way to reach thirty words. Point two, what you do at home."),
            jonas("Ich lerne zu Hause die Wörter und mache die Hausaufgaben. [repeat]", "At home I learn the words and do the homework."),
            leo("And point three, when I come back."),
            maya("A time word first, then the verb in second place, then «ich»."),
            jonas("Nächsten Samstag komme ich wieder. [repeat]", "I'll be back next Saturday."),
            leo("That's it? Then «Mit freundlichen Grüßen» and my name?"),
            maya("That's it. Five lines, about thirty words, three points covered. Now the friendly version. Your friend Tom has a birthday. Write: congratulations, what you're giving him, and when you're coming. Congratulations is «Glückwunsch»."),
            jonas("Lieber Tom, herzlichen Glückwunsch zum Geburtstag! [repeat]", "Dear Tom, happy birthday!"),
            leo("«Herzlichen» has a small letter there. Because of the comma?"),
            maya("Exactly, the comma rule again. Then the gift and the time, with «dir», to you, because Tom is a friend."),
            jonas("Ich schenke dir ein Buch über Fußball. Ich komme am Samstag um sechs Uhr. [repeat]", "I'm giving you a book about football. I'm coming on Saturday at six."),
            leo("Are there sentences I can reuse in almost any task?"),
            maya("A handful. To ask for something: «Kannst du mir bitte» for a friend, «Können Sie mir bitte» for Frau Braun. To say sorry: «Es tut mir leid». To say thanks: «Vielen Dank für». And a question is the easiest third point."),
            jonas("Können Sie mir bitte die Hausaufgaben schicken? [repeat]", "Can you please send me the homework?"),
            leo("And if Lena invites me to her party?"),
            maya("[laugh] Then you go, obviously. Thank her for the invitation, say when you're coming, and ask a question."),
            jonas("Vielen Dank für die Einladung. Soll ich etwas mitbringen? [repeat]", "Thank you for the invitation. Should I bring anything?"),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("For «Schreiben», part two: five lines, greeting, point, point, point, sign-off, about thirty words. «Du» goes with «Liebe» and «Viele Grüße»; «Sie» goes with «Sehr geehrte» and «Mit freundlichen Grüßen». And check all three content points are there before you hand it in."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Write one message in twenty minutes, then mark it against its own three points. Five lines: greeting, three points, sign-off. About thirty words. Keep the greeting and the sign-off in the same register, and check each content point is actually there."),
            jonas("Viele Grüße und bis bald!", "Best wishes, and see you soon!"),
            leo("«Bis bald», then. See you next time."),
        ),
        series = PodcastSeries.GERMAN_A1,
    )

    private val de11Reading = Podcast(
        "de11-reading",
        listOf(
            maya("Welcome back. Today is «Lesen», the reading paper. Twenty-five minutes, fifteen points, three parts. You've met all three in the course already, so today is the method for beating each one."),
            leo("A method for reading? I usually just... read."),
            maya("[chuckle] That's the first mistake. In «Teil eins», part one, you get two short personal texts, emails or notes, and statements to mark «richtig» or «falsch», true or false. Read the statements first, then the text. Each statement maps to one sentence. Here's an email: Usaid is moving house, and he writes to Tom."),
            jonas("Hallo Tom, am Samstag ziehen wir um! [repeat]", "Hi Tom, we're moving on Saturday!"),
            leo("«Ziehen wir um». So «umziehen» splits, like the other verbs that come apart?"),
            maya("Yes. «Umziehen», to move house, splits: «ziehen» in second place, «um» at the very end. Now the statement: Usaid moves on Sunday. «Richtig» or «falsch»?"),
            leo("«Falsch». The email says Saturday."),
            maya("Right. Day words, times and people flip the answer: «Samstag» or «Sonntag», «ich» or «wir». So do «nicht» and «kein». And if the text doesn't mention it at all, it's «falsch» at A1. Here's more of Usaid's email."),
            jonas("Kannst du uns helfen? Bring bitte dein Auto mit. [repeat]", "Can you help us? Please bring your car."),
            leo("So the statement, Tom should bring his car. That's «richtig»?"),
            maya("«Richtig», and you can point at the exact line. That's the test: every «richtig» needs a line you can point at. On to «Teil zwei»: five situations, two adverts each. An advert is «die Anzeige», and you pick the one that fits."),
            jonas("Welche Anzeige passt? [repeat]", "Which advert fits?"),
            leo("Sounds easy. Find the one about swimming, done."),
            maya("[laugh] That's exactly the trap. Both adverts match the topic. The wrong one fails one detail: closed on Sunday, too expensive, the wrong city. So underline the two or three conditions in the situation: what, when, how much, where. Here's the situation."),
            jonas("Sie möchten am Sonntag mit Ihren Kindern schwimmen gehen. [repeat]", "You want to go swimming with your children on Sunday."),
            leo("Conditions: Sunday, children, swimming."),
            maya("Good. Advert a is «Schwimmbad Nord». Open Monday to Saturday, from seven in the morning, and then this."),
            jonas("Sonntag geschlossen. [repeat]", "Closed on Sunday."),
            leo("Out, then? Even though it's a pool?"),
            maya("Out. One miss and it's gone. It's the right topic, but it fails the day. Advert b is «Freizeitbad Süd»."),
            jonas("Täglich neun bis zwanzig Uhr. Kinderbecken. Familienkarte fünfzehn Euro.", "Open daily, nine to eight. Children's pool. Family ticket fifteen euros."),
            leo("Every day, and a children's pool. So it's b."),
            maya("It's b: every condition holds. Now «Teil drei»: five signs and notices, and again «richtig» or «falsch». A sign is «das Schild». Learn the sign words first. They're short, and you'll see them on every door in Germany."),
            jonas("Eingang, Ausgang, Kasse. Drücken, ziehen. [repeat]", "Entrance, exit, till. Push, pull."),
            leo("«Drücken» is push and «ziehen» is pull? I'll get that wrong on every door."),
            maya("Everyone does, once. Then come the rules: what's forbidden, what's allowed, and who it's for."),
            jonas("Rauchen verboten. Hunde erlaubt. Parken nur für Kunden. [repeat]", "No smoking. Dogs allowed. Parking for customers only."),
            leo("So the statement says anyone may park here. That's «falsch»?"),
            maya("«Falsch». «Nur», only, flips it, just like «nicht», «kein», «geschlossen» and «verboten». The other trap is numbers. Here's a sign on a library door, with the opening hours."),
            jonas("Öffnungszeiten: Samstag zehn bis vierzehn Uhr. [repeat]", "Opening hours: Saturday ten to two."),
            leo("And the statement says, on Saturday you can borrow books at three in the afternoon."),
            maya("«Falsch»: it shuts at two. And the statement is often a rewording. The sign says «Essen und Trinken verboten». The statement says you may not eat in the library. That's «richtig». The sign also says you need a «Bibliotheksausweis», a library card, to borrow books."),
            jonas("Man darf in der Bibliothek nicht essen. [repeat]", "You may not eat in the library."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("Three things for «Lesen». «Teil eins»: statements first, and watch the day words and the negations. «Teil zwei»: every condition must hold; the decoy fails one detail. «Teil drei»: learn the sign words, and watch for rewordings and numbers."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Sit a whole «Lesen» paper under the real clock: all three parts in twenty-five minutes. Underline the condition in each part two situation. Check every «richtig» against a line you can point at. Then mark it, and write the reason for each miss."),
            jonas("Viel Glück beim Lesen! Tschüss!", "Good luck with the reading! Bye!"),
            leo("Good luck, everyone. See you next time."),
        ),
        series = PodcastSeries.GERMAN_A1,
    )

    private val de12Overview = Podcast(
        "de12-overview",
        listOf(
            maya("Welcome to module twelve, the last stop before the exam. Today is the exam itself: the Goethe A1, its four papers, how the points add up, and what sixty out of a hundred really means. Jonas will give us the instructions you'll read and hear on the day, so they sound familiar when it counts."),
            leo("So is it one big exam, or lots of little ones?"),
            maya("Four papers. Three are written, in one sitting of about sixty-five minutes. The fourth is speaking, in a group. Jonas, the four names, please."),
            jonas("Hören, Lesen, Schreiben, Sprechen. [repeat]", "Listening, reading, writing, speaking."),
            leo("How long is each one?"),
            maya("«Hören», listening, is about twenty minutes. «Lesen», reading, is twenty-five. «Schreiben», writing, is twenty. And «Sprechen», speaking, is fifteen minutes, with up to four candidates and two examiners. The German word for a candidate is a long one: «Prüfungsteilnehmer», someone taking part in the exam."),
            leo("[chuckle] Of course it is. And the points?"),
            maya("Picture four jars, each holding twenty-five points. Whatever you score in a paper is scaled to fit its jar, so the four together make a hundred. You pass with sixty in total. «Gut», good, starts at eighty, and «sehr gut», very good, at ninety."),
            leo("Do I need sixty in every paper?"),
            maya("No, and that's the most useful fact today. There's no minimum in any one paper. Say you score ten in «Hören», twenty in «Lesen», eighteen in «Schreiben» and fourteen in «Sprechen». Add them up: sixty-two. That's a pass. A strong paper carries a weak one."),
            leo("So I should practise the paper I like best?"),
            maya("The opposite. The total is what passes, so an hour on your weak paper is worth more points than an hour on the one you already enjoy. Now, the day itself. You register with your passport, and phones go off and away. «Hören» comes first, with a pencil and an answer sheet."),
            jonas("Schreiben Sie auf den Antwortbogen. [repeat]", "Write on the answer sheet."),
            leo("Why does the answer sheet get its own special word?"),
            maya("Because only the answer sheet is marked. You also get a booklet with the questions, and you can scribble in it, but nobody reads it. In «Hören» there are pauses, and those pauses are for moving your answers across. It's «der Antwortbogen»."),
            leo("Got it. What else will the paper say to me?"),
            maya("Every part starts with an instruction, and they're always polite, with «Sie». First: read the task."),
            jonas("Lesen Sie die Aufgabe. [repeat]", "Read the task."),
            leo("«Die Aufgabe», the task. And then?"),
            maya("Then there's usually a worked example at the top, so you can see what they want. That's «das Beispiel». Look at it before you start."),
            jonas("Hier ist ein Beispiel. [repeat]", "Here is an example."),
            leo("And when I know the answer?"),
            maya("You mark it. The answer is «die Lösung», literally the solution."),
            jonas("Markieren Sie die Lösung. [repeat]", "Mark the answer."),
            leo("Mark it how? A circle? A tick?"),
            maya("Usually a cross in a box. «Kreuzen Sie an» means tick, put your cross in. And in «Lesen» the boxes are often «richtig» or «falsch», true or false."),
            jonas("Kreuzen Sie an: Richtig oder Falsch. [repeat]", "Tick: true or false."),
            leo("What about «Schreiben»?"),
            maya("Two parts. First a form with five gaps, where you fill in the missing details. Then a short message of about thirty words, covering three points, in full sentences. The instruction for the form uses «ergänzen», to complete."),
            jonas("Ergänzen Sie das Formular. [repeat]", "Complete the form."),
            leo("And in the listening, do I get to hear things more than once?"),
            maya("Mostly, yes, and the paper tells you. Listen for this sentence before each part. If it says twice, you can relax a little on the first hearing. One part is played only once, and the next episode is all about that."),
            jonas("Sie hören den Text zweimal. [repeat]", "You hear the text twice."),
            leo("[sigh] And then the speaking. In a group. With strangers watching."),
            maya("It's often on a different day, with up to four of you and two examiners. The examiner is «der Prüfer», or «die Prüferin» for a woman. They give the instructions in German, show the cards, and ask the questions."),
            jonas("Die Prüferin stellt Fragen. [repeat]", "The examiner asks questions."),
            leo("And after all that, when do I find out?"),
            maya("Results come in about a week. The «Zertifikat», the certificate, arrives by post, with your grade on it. Sixty is a pass."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("Three things. «Hören», «Lesen», «Schreiben» and «Sprechen» are worth twenty-five each, and sixty in total passes, with no minimum per paper, so revise your weakest. In «Hören», move your answers to the «Antwortbogen» in the pauses, because the booklet isn't marked. And bring your passport and a pencil."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Ten minutes with the exam's own numbers. Write down the four papers and their minutes. Say out loud what sixty of a hundred means for your weakest paper. Find your nearest exam centre, and check what ID you would need to register. Then you know the whole day before it starts."),
            jonas("Viel Glück! Bis bald!", "Good luck! See you soon!"),
            leo("Good luck to all of us. See you next time."),
        ),
        series = PodcastSeries.GERMAN_A1,
    )

    private val de12Listening = Podcast(
