            jonas("Wann kommt der Zug? [repeat]", "When does the train come?"),
            leo("«Wann», when. So I'm listening for a time."),
            maya("Exactly. A time, so your ears go to «Uhr», «halb», «Viertel» and numbers. Everything else can wash past you. If the question asks where, you listen for a place. If it asks how much, a price."),
            leo("Okay. And the three parts?"),
            maya("«Teil eins» is six short dialogues, each played twice, with three pictures or options. On the first hearing, get the gist and pick a likely answer. On the second, check the detail. Here's one. A woman asks when the next bus to the station leaves, and the man answers."),
            jonas("Um zehn nach acht. Nein, warten Sie, am Sonntag erst um halb neun. [repeat]", "At ten past eight. No, wait, on Sunday not until half past eight."),
            leo("Hang on. He said two times. Which one counts?"),
            maya("The second one. This is the paper's favourite trick: speakers correct themselves on purpose, like «um neun, nein, um halb zehn». The first number is the one being taken back, and it's exactly the one the options will offer you. So never tick the first number you hear. Wait for the correction."),
            leo("Sneaky. What's the other trick?"),
            maya("A word from a wrong option is nearly always mentioned somewhere. Sanya asks Tom if they can meet on Saturday. Listen to his answer."),
            jonas("Samstag kann ich nicht, ich arbeite. Aber Sonntag um drei im Park? [repeat]", "I can't on Saturday, I'm working. But Sunday at three in the park?"),
            leo("So Saturday is mentioned, but the answer is Sunday."),
            maya("Right. Hearing a word doesn't make its option right. Ask yourself: does it answer the question? They meet «Sonntag um drei». Now «Teil zwei». That's four public announcements, at a station or in a shop, and you hear each one only once. An announcement is «eine Durchsage». You decide «richtig» or «falsch», true or false."),
            leo("Only once? [sigh] That's the scary one."),
            maya("It is, so the reading window is your whole strategy. Say the statement is: the train leaves from platform two. The detail type is a platform, so listen just for that. And watch for «nicht … sondern», not this but that. Whatever comes after «sondern» is the answer."),
            jonas("Der Zug fährt heute nicht von Gleis zwei, sondern von Gleis sechs. [repeat]", "Today the train doesn't leave from platform two, but from platform six."),
            leo("So it's platform six, and the statement is false."),
            maya("Yes, «falsch». The word two was said, and it's still wrong. Here's the pattern on its own, with times."),
            jonas("Nicht um neun, sondern um zehn. [repeat]", "Not at nine, but at ten."),
            leo("What other words do announcements love?"),
            maya("Two small ones. At the supermarket, apples are on offer, and the offer is valid only today. To be valid is «gelten»."),
            jonas("Das Angebot gilt nur heute. [repeat]", "The offer is valid today only."),
            leo("And the other one?"),
            maya("«Bereits», already. At the museum, the announcement says it closes at five, and the ticket desk is already closed. So if the statement says you can still buy a ticket, that's «falsch»."),
            jonas("Die Kasse ist bereits geschlossen. [repeat]", "The ticket desk is already closed."),
            leo("And «Teil drei»?"),
            maya("Five phone messages, each played twice, with three options. The options are usually three times, days or places. Note what you hear the first time, then check it on the second. Here's a message for Usaid from Dr Müller's practice."),
            jonas("Ihr Termin ist nicht am Dienstag um neun, sondern am Mittwoch um neun. [repeat]", "Your appointment is not on Tuesday at nine, but on Wednesday at nine."),
            leo("There it is again, «nicht … sondern». So, Wednesday."),
            maya("Wednesday. And messages often end with a request: if the time doesn't work, please call back. To call back is «zurückrufen»."),
            jonas("Bitte rufen Sie zurück. [repeat]", "Please call back."),
            leo("One more?"),
            maya("The language school calls. The Monday course moves an hour later, and to a different room. Listen for «später», later, and for the room number."),
            jonas("Der Kurs am Montag beginnt eine Stunde später, um neunzehn Uhr, in Raum vier. [repeat]", "The Monday course starts an hour later, at seven in the evening, in room four."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the Goethe exam?"),
            maya("Three things for «Hören». Read the question first and decide the detail type: a time, a place, a price. With corrections and «nicht … sondern», the second thing is the answer. And move your answers to the answer sheet in the pauses, because only the «Antwortbogen» is marked."),
